# Clean Architecture Implementation

## Layer Structure
The project follows Clean Architecture with clear separation of concerns:

### Domain Layer (`src/domain/`)
- Contains core business entities and rules
- No external dependencies
- Pure business logic
- Example: [chatHistory.js](mdc:backend/src/domain/chatHistory.js)

### Application Layer (`src/application/`)
- Contains use cases and application services
- Orchestrates domain objects
- Depends only on domain layer
- Example: [chatService.js](mdc:backend/src/application/chatService.js)

### Infrastructure Layer (`src/infrastructure/`)
- Contains external service implementations
- Handles technical concerns (APIs, databases, etc.)
- Implements interfaces defined in application layer
- Examples: [ollamaClient.js](mdc:backend/src/infrastructure/ollamaClient.js), [seleniumService.js](mdc:backend/src/infrastructure/seleniumService.js)

### Presentation Layer (`src/server.js`)
- Contains HTTP endpoints and request handling
- Depends on application layer
- Handles serialization/deserialization

## Dependency Direction
- Dependencies point inward (toward domain)
- Domain layer has no dependencies
- Application layer depends only on domain
- Infrastructure implements application interfaces
- Presentation orchestrates application services

## Service Pattern
```javascript
class ChatService {
    constructor() {
        // Inject infrastructure dependencies
        this.ollamaClient = new OllamaClient();
        this.chatHistory = new ChatHistory();
        this.seleniumService = new SeleniumService();
    }
    
    // Application use cases
    async sendMessage(userInput) {
        // Orchestrate domain and infrastructure
    }
}
```

## Domain Entity Pattern
```javascript
class ChatHistory {
    constructor() {
        this.messages = [];
    }
    
    addMessage(role, content) {
        // Pure business logic
    }
    
    getMessages() {
        // Domain operations
    }
}
```

## Benefits
- Testability: Easy to mock dependencies
- Maintainability: Clear separation of concerns
- Flexibility: Easy to swap implementations
- Scalability: Well-defined boundaries